			"date": "2026-05-12T01:27:05.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> pull_request_target strikes again!</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "116562029832306312",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat/116562029832306312",
			"author": {
				"name": "Zach Leatherman",
				"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/accounts/avatars/109/286/461/031/266/152/original/dc489c3746a44e00.png"
			},
			"date": "2026-05-12T14:17:05.905Z",
			"content": "<p>A follow up here on action items (assuming you’re already using trusted publishers OIDC to scope releases to a single GitHub Action workflow):</p><p>1. Look for any `pull_request_target` GitHub Actions workflows! (this allows external forks/code to run your actions with write access ☠️☠️☠️☠️☠️)<br />2. Look for use of `cache` in your GitHub Actions release workflow (cache was poisoned/compromised by `pull_request_target` trigger)</p><p>Learn more about `pull_request_target`: <a href=\"https://securitylab.github.com/resources/github-actions-preventing-pwn-requests/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" translate=\"no\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">securitylab.github.com/resourc</span><span class=\"invisible\">es/github-actions-preventing-pwn-requests/</span></a></p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "116562081436896858",
			"parentId": "116562029832306312",
			"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat/116562081436896858",
			"author": {
				"name": "Zach Leatherman",
				"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/accounts/avatars/109/286/461/031/266/152/original/dc489c3746a44e00.png"
			},
			"date": "2026-05-12T14:30:13.329Z",
			"content": "<p>Importantly, `pull_request_target` IGNORES this GitHub security setting: <a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/enabling-features-for-your-repository/managing-github-actions-settings-for-a-repository#controlling-changes-from-forks-to-workflows-in-public-repositories\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" translate=\"no\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">docs.github.com/en/repositorie</span><span class=\"invisible\">s/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/enabling-features-for-your-repository/managing-github-actions-settings-for-a-repository#controlling-changes-from-forks-to-workflows-in-public-repositories</span></a></p><p>This was very surprising to me.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
